  </div>
  <p>
    MWM does not claim to model all of mathematics. It focuses specifically on the mathematics supporting <strong>mathematical physics</strong>, balancing two design parameters:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><em>What physical theories can be modeled?</em></li>
    <li><em>At what level of depth can the modeling mathematics be understood?</em></li>
  </ol>

  <h2>2. The 2-Successor Tree and the Cornerstone Axiom</h2>

  <p>
    MWM expresses its concepts formally, embedded in natural language explanations, using single-sorted first-order logic and ZF set theory as its foundational baseline.
  </p>

  <p>
    MWM operates as an <strong>effective model</strong>: rather than building foundational set-theoretic proofs from scratch, it asserts established mathematical structures as governing axioms. The cornerstone axiom of MWM is:
  </p>

  <div class="axiom-box">
    <strong>(No(ε₀), +, ·) is a totally ordered algebraic field</strong>
  </div>

  <p>where:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The carrier set <code>No(ε₀)</code> consists of nodes on the <strong>2-successor tree</strong> whose tree depth (birthday) is strictly less than the transfinite ordinal <code>ε₀</code>.</li>
    <li>The 2-successor tree is a rooted binary tree where every branch splits into two directed choices, conventionally denoted <code>[-]</code> (left) and <code>[+]</code> (right).</li>
    <li>The total order (<code>≤</code>) and the arithmetic operations (<code>+</code>, <code>·</code>) are defined recursively with respect to the node's stage of creation.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Because <code>No(ε₀)</code> is <strong>non-Archimedean</strong>, standard ε–δ analysis is set aside in favor of a <strong>constructive nonstandard analysis</strong>. <code>No(ε₀)</code> serves as the ambient field for all arithmetic and continuous modeling.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Numbers Indexed by Finite Ordinals &amp; The Dyadic Isomorphism</h2>

  <p>
    Ordinals (<code>Ord</code>) index the growth stages of the 2-successor tree:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The natural numbers <code>ℕ</code> are identified with the finite ordinals.</li>
    <li><code>ω</code> denotes the supremum of all finite ordinals.</li>
    <li><code>ε₀</code> bounds our transfinite universe, with <code>ω + 1 &lt; ε₀</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For any ordinal <code>α</code>, <code>No(α)</code> denotes the set of all 2-successor tree nodes whose depth is less than <code>α</code>. MWM establishes two key operational stages:
  </p>

  <h3>A. Finite-Stage Numbers: No(ω)</h3>

  <p>
    A node created at a finite ordinal stage <code>n &lt; ω</code> is uniquely identified by a finite sequence of binary branch choices of length <code>n</code>:
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <code class="math-formula">s = (s₁, s₂, …, sₙ), &nbsp; sᵢ ∈ { -, + }</code>
  </div>

  <p>
    Inheriting the field operations of <code>No(ε₀)</code>, the finite-stage numbers form a commutative ring:
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <code class="math-formula">(No(ω), +, ·)</code>
  </div>

  <p>Every finite sign sequence maps directly to a sum of powers of 2. For example:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The root <code>∅</code> at stage 0 is <code>0</code>.</li>
    <li>At stage 1: <code>[+]</code> is <code>+1</code>, and <code>[-]</code> is <code>-1</code>.</li>
    <li>At stage 2: <code>[+ -]</code> is <code>+1/2</code>, and <code>[+ +]</code> is <code>+2</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    MWM demonstrates that this ring is strictly isomorphic to the ring of <strong>dyadic rationals</strong>:
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <code class="math-formula">(No(ω), +, ·) ≅ (𝔻, +, ·)</code><br>
    <code class="math-formula">𝔻 = { m / 2ᵏ &nbsp;∣&nbsp; m ∈ ℤ, &nbsp;k ∈ ℕ }</code>
  </div>

  <p>
    Consequently, <strong><code>𝔻</code> becomes the arena of all concrete numeric calculation</strong>. Computation in MWM takes place within a <strong>Principal Ideal Domain (PID)</strong> rather than a field, directly reflecting how discrete digital systems perform exact arithmetic.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Transfinite Paths: No(ω+1) and Precision Evaluation</h2>

  <p>
    MWM does not assign an independent algebraic structure to the carrier set <code>No(ω+1)</code>. Instead, <code>No(ω+1)</code> is used as a <strong>syntactic vocabulary</strong> for algebraic expressions:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>It includes all finite nodes <code>No(ω)</code> together with nodes created at stage <code>ω</code> (infinite binary paths).</li>
    <li>All standard real numbers exist in <code>No(ω+1)</code> (<code>ℝ ⊂ No(ω+1)</code>).</li>
    <li>In addition to <code>ℝ</code>, <code>No(ω+1)</code> naturally contains transfinite numbers (such as <code>ω = [+, +, +, …]</code>) and infinitesimals (such as <code>dx = [+ - - …] ≈ 1/ω</code>).</li>
    <li>All such quantities exist in the ambient field <code>No(ε₀)</code>, ensuring valid algebraic manipulation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>The Precision Evaluation Function</h3>

  <p>
    To evaluate an algebraic expression <code>E</code> whose terms belong to <code>No(ω+1)</code>, MWM defines a computable truncation map:
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <code class="math-formula">eval(E, p)</code>
  </div>

  <p>Given a target precision <code>p ∈ ℕ</code>:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Each number node in <code>E</code> is replaced by its 2-successor tree address truncated to the first <code>p</code> directional choices, yielding an exact element of <code>𝔻</code>.</li>
    <li>Infinitesimal terms are bounded and resolved, reproducing the standard part map (<code>st</code>) of nonstandard analysis.</li>
    <li>The expression resolves to an exact dyadic quantity in <code>𝔻</code> at precision <code>p</code>.</li>
  </ol>

